Subject: software ISP: don't queue work to a stopped worker thread

SoftwareIsp::stop() stops the debayer worker thread and then calls
ipa_->stop(), a synchronous IPC call that spins a nested event loop while it
waits. That loop can deliver one more captured frame, which reaches
SimpleCameraData::imageBufferReady() -> SoftwareIsp::queueBuffers() ->
process() and posts a Debayer::process message to the worker that was just
stopped. The message stays in the worker's queue and is run by the next
start(), on input/output buffers that have since been released, crashing
DebayerCpu::process() in Debayer::dmaSyncBegin() (SIGSEGV). Applications
that stop one camera and start another quickly (GNOME Camera switching
rear/front) trigger it reliably.

Skip posting when the worker isn't running. The buffers were already added
to queuedInputBuffers_/queuedOutputBuffers_ by queueBuffers(), and stop()
cancels everything in those lists once the IPA is stopped.

diff --git a/src/libcamera/software_isp/software_isp.cpp b/src/libcamera/software_isp/software_isp.cpp
index 321d05d46..4ce22d473 100644
--- a/src/libcamera/software_isp/software_isp.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/software_isp/software_isp.cpp
@@ -432,6 +432,17 @@ void SoftwareIsp::stop()
  */
 void SoftwareIsp::process(uint32_t frame, FrameBuffer *input, FrameBuffer *output)
 {
+	/*
+	 * stop() halts the worker thread and then synchronously stops the IPA.
+	 * That IPC call runs a nested event loop, which can deliver one more
+	 * captured frame and reach this function. Don't post work to a stopped
+	 * worker: the message would stay queued and be run by the next start()
+	 * on buffers that no longer exist. The buffers are already in the
+	 * queued lists at this point, and stop() cancels them afterwards.
+	 */
+	if (!ispWorkerThread_.isRunning())
+		return;
+
 	ipa_->computeParams(frame);
 	debayer_->invokeMethod(&Debayer::process,
 			       ConnectionTypeQueued, frame, input, output, debayerParams_);
